@page "/guide"

<PageTitle>CardGameEngine — Step-by-step guide</PageTitle>

<MudContainer MaxWidth="MaxWidth.Large" Class="py-8">
    <MudStack Spacing="6">
        <MudText Typo="Typo.h3">Step-by-step guide</MudText>
        <MudText>
            This guide is intentionally derived from the CardGameEngine implementation in the
            <strong>fix/misc</strong> branch. The examples use only constructors, methods and interfaces
            that exist in that implementation.
        </MudText>

        <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info">
            The complete executable example is
            <code>examples/CardGameEngine.Demo/Program.cs</code>. Use it as the canonical reference
            when adapting these snippets to a real game.
        </MudAlert>

        <MudStepper Vertical="true">
            <MudStep Title="1. Reference CardGameEngine source">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    Use the CardGameEngine project from this repository while the NuGet package is unpublished.
                    CGE contains the game model and execution pipeline; it does not require a particular UI framework.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>dotnet new console -n MyCardGame
cd MyCardGame
dotnet add reference ../../src/CardGameEngine/CardGameEngine.csproj</code></pre>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="2. Create players and the game">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    Create the participants and pass them to <code>Game</code>. The game tracks the players
                    and the active player. The constructor expects at least one player because it selects
                    an initial active-player index.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>using CardGameEngine;

var player = new Player();
var opponent = new Player();

var game = new Game(new List&lt;IPlayer&gt;
{
    player,
    opponent
});

game.ActivePlayer = player;</code></pre>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="3. Build the decks and start the game">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    A <code>Deck</code> is a stack. Add cards with <code>Push</code>; the top card is returned
                    by <code>Pop</code>. Add the cards needed by your game before calling <code>StartGame</code>.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>var monster = new MonsterCard(
    2, 5, 6, player, "Knight");

var opponentMonster = new MonsterCard(
    1, 3, 4, opponent, "Goblin");

player.Deck.Push(monster);
opponent.Deck.Push(opponentMonster);

game.StartGame(
    initialHandSize: 1,
    initialPlayerLife: 20);</code></pre>
                <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                    Because the deck is a stack, the last card pushed is the first card drawn. The repository
                    demo explicitly pushes cards in reverse draw order.
                </MudAlert>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="4. Create monster cards">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    Use <code>MonsterCard</code> for a board character with mana, attack and life.
                    You can use it directly or derive a game-specific class from it.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>public sealed class KnightCard : MonsterCard
{
    public KnightCard(IPlayer owner)
        : base(
            mana: 2,
            attack: 5,
            life: 6,
            owner: owner,
            name: "Knight")
    {
    }
}

var knight = new KnightCard(player);</code></pre>
                <MudText Class="mt-4">
                    A monster is played through <code>Player.CastMonster</code>. The corresponding action
                    requires the player to be active, the card to be in the player's hand, the card to be
                    summonable and the board slot to be free.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>player.CastMonster(game, knight, boardIndex: 0);</code></pre>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="5. Create a targetless spell">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    A <code>TargetlessSpellCard</code> has one or more components derived from
                    <code>TargetlessSpellCardComponent</code>. The component receives the game when the
                    spell resolves and can issue normal engine actions.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>public sealed class HealComponent : TargetlessSpellCardComponent
{
    public HealComponent(int mana)
        : base(mana)
    {
    }

    public override void Cast(IGame game)
    {
        game.Execute(
            new ModifyLifeStatAction(
                game.ActivePlayer,
                3));
    }

    public override object Clone() =>
        new HealComponent(ManaValue);
}

var heal = new TargetlessSpellCard(
    new HealComponent(1),
    player,
    "Heal");</code></pre>
                <pre><code>player.CastSpell(game, heal);</code></pre>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="6. Create a targetful spell">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    A <code>TargetfulSpellCard</code> requires a target. Its component must implement
                    both the effect and the valid-target calculation for the current game state.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>public sealed class LightningComponent : TargetfulSpellCardComponent
{
    public LightningComponent(int mana)
        : base(mana)
    {
    }

    public override void Cast(
        IGame game,
        ICharacter target)
    {
        game.Execute(
            new ModifyLifeStatAction(
                target,
                -4));
    }

    public override HashSet&lt;ICharacter&gt; GetPotentialTargets(
        IGameState gameState)
    {
        var targets = new HashSet&lt;ICharacter&gt;();

        foreach (var opponent in gameState.NonActivePlayers)
        {
            foreach (var character in opponent.Characters)
            {
                targets.Add(character);
            }
        }

        return targets;
    }

    public override object Clone() =>
        new LightningComponent(ManaValue);
}

var lightning = new TargetfulSpellCard(
    new LightningComponent(2),
    player,
    "Lightning");</code></pre>
                <pre><code>player.CastSpell(
    game,
    lightning,
    opponent);</code></pre>
                <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                    The targetful cast action checks that the selected target belongs to the component's
                    current <code>GetPotentialTargets</code> result. Do not keep a stale target list.
                </MudAlert>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="7. Use actions for state changes">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    <code>IAction</code> is the unit of state transition. Every action provides
                    <code>IsExecutable(IGameState)</code> and <code>Execute(IGame)</code>. The action queue
                    checks the current state immediately before execution.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>game.Execute(
    new ModifyManaStatAction(
        player,
        4,
        4));

game.Execute(
    new DrawCardAction(player));</code></pre>
                <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Warning" Class="mt-4">
                    An action that is no longer executable is skipped. For example, <code>DrawCardAction</code>
                    requires a non-empty deck and a hand with available capacity.
                </MudAlert>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="8. Use events and reactions">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    Events are actions used as lifecycle markers. Reactions observe the resulting
                    <code>IActionEvent</code> and can issue additional actions through <code>game.Execute</code>.
                    CGE includes built-in reactions for turn changes, mana, drawing and monster readiness.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>public sealed class ExampleReaction : Reaction
{
    public override void ReactTo(
        IGame game,
        IActionEvent actionEvent)
    {
        if (actionEvent.IsAfter(
            typeof(StartOfTurnEvent)))
        {
            game.Execute(
                new ModifyLifeStatAction(
                    game.ActivePlayer,
                    1));
        }
    }

    public override object Clone() =>
        new ExampleReaction(); 
}</code></pre>
                <MudText Class="mt-4">
                    The actual <code>IReaction</code> contract is <code>ReactTo(IGame, IActionEvent)</code>;
                    reactions do not return an <code>IEnumerable&lt;IAction&gt;</code>. Attach reactions to the
                    reactive objects used by the engine so that <code>AllReactions()</code> can discover them.
                </MudText>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="9. Advance turns and combat">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    Use <code>NextTurn()</code> to process the end of the current turn and the beginning
                    of the next one. Then a ready monster on the active player's board can attack a valid target.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>game.NextTurn();

game.NextTurn();

// The monster must be on the active player's board
// and ready to attack.
monster.Attack(game, opponent);</code></pre>
                <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                    <code>AttackAction</code> applies damage to both characters and marks the attacker as
                    not ready. It does not call <code>NextTurn()</code> automatically.
                </MudAlert>
            </MudStep>

            <MudStep Title="10. Clone, end and validate the game">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
                <MudText Class="mb-4">
                    Clone the current game when you need an independent state snapshot. To mark the game
                    as over, execute <code>EndOfGameEvent</code>. After that, the action queue skips later actions.
                </MudText>
                <pre><code>var clone = (Game)game.Clone();

game.Execute(new EndOfGameEvent());

// Actions submitted after EndOfGameEvent are not executed.
game.Execute(new DrawCardAction(player));</code></pre>
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mt-6">Build and test</MudText>
                <pre><code>dotnet build CGE.slnx -c Release
dotnet test CGE.slnx -c Release
dotnet run --project examples/CardGameEngine.Demo/CardGameEngine.Demo.csproj</code></pre>
                <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                    The repository tests specifically cover the action invariants fixed in this branch:
                    full-hand draws, inactive-player casts, cards outside the hand, invalid spell targets
                    and attackers that are not on the active player's board.
                </MudAlert>
            </MudStep>
        </MudStepper>
    </MudStack>
</MudContainer>
